Hyperemesis Gravidarum
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Intractable vomiting, Weight loss >5%, Ketonuria

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-022
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting vomiting in early pregnancy resulting in severe orthostatic dizziness and an inability to tolerate any oral intake, requiring urgent after-hours assessment for severe dehydration and electrolyte derangement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a pregnant patient (10 weeks gestation) with intractable vomiting occurring over 15 times today, resulting in complete anuria for 12 hours and severe dizziness upon standing. This mandated an urgent home assessment to evaluate intravascular volume and initiate parenteral antiemetics, preventing progression to severe maternal and fetal compromise prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Uncontrollable vomiting, severe dizziness, and extreme exhaustion. Patient states, I vomit even if I just swallow my own saliva, and the room spins violently when I try to stand up.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have escalated severely over the past two weeks, presenting as intractable nausea and vomiting occurring multiple times daily before 20 weeks of gestation. The patient reports a documented unintended weight loss exceeding 5 percent of pre-pregnancy body weight. Today, the patient has been unable to retain any fluids. There is pronounced orthostatic hypotension and dizziness upon standing. The patient describes severely dry mucous membranes and excessive persistent thirst. Ptyalism (excessive salivation) is prominent, prompting frequent spitting as swallowing exacerbates the nausea. The patient notes reduced urine output (oliguria), passing only a minimal amount of highly concentrated, dark urine early this morning. Extreme fatigue, lethargy, and physical exhaustion are overwhelming.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe nausea and orthostatic dizziness; heavily reliant on partner for assistance.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncope and severe electrolyte imbalance; partner is highly supportive but distressed by the symptom severity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 58.0 Kg (Pre-pregnancy weight 62.0 Kg)
General: Patient appears frail, acutely exhausted, pale, and is holding an emesis basin continuously.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked, reflecting significant intravascular volume depletion. Postural blood pressure check deferred due to severe nausea, but historical orthostasis is profound. Capillary refill is delayed (3 seconds). Respiratory: Clear to auscultation. Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft, non-tender, without focal rigidity or masses. Uterus is non-palpable abdominally (consistent with early gestation). Integumentary/Genitourinary: The diagnostic triad is clinically confirmed: intractable vomiting is witnessed, weight loss >5% is documented, and point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates significant ketonuria (+++), indicating severe starvation metabolism. Examination reveals poor skin turgor with generalized loss of skin elasticity and deeply dry oral mucosa.
Environmental Observations: Room is kept dark and quiet; strong odors have been removed by the partner to minimize nausea triggers.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has failed oral trials of Doxylamine/Pyridoxine and Metoclopramide.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hyperemesis Gravidarum - Status: Acute Decompensation
Differentials:

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prolonged, progressive chronicity of symptoms correlated with early gestation, the complete absence of diarrhea, and the lack of fever.

Urinary Tract Infection/Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dysuria, flank pain, or fever, and urinalysis negative for nitrites and leukocytes (positive only for specific gravity and ketones).

Molar Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prior early viable ultrasound confirming a singleton intrauterine pregnancy, though the severe symptoms warrant repeat obstetric review.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from severe hyperemesis gravidarum, resulting in critical gastrointestinal fluid loss, starvation ketosis, and overt clinical hypovolemia. The presence of significant tachycardia, anuria, and >5% body weight loss indicates a highly decompensated state. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in severe electrolyte derangements (hypokalemia, hypochloremia) and potential maternal cardiovascular collapse or fetal distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was strictly necessary to confirm the severity of the dehydration, rule out acute surgical or infectious abdominal pathologies, and administer immediate parenteral antiemetic therapy to safely attempt oral rehydration or coordinate immediate hospital admission for intravenous fluids. The expected investigation results include significant ketonuria on urinalysis with a hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis on serum chemistry that would assist in confirming the diagnosis formally.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually. Provided a second dose of Ondansetron 4mg as a wafer for use in 8 hours. Directed the partner to initiate micro-sips of an ice-cold electrolyte solution once the severe nausea abated.
Positive Behavioral Change: To bypass the nausea reflex and begin replacing fluid, commit to taking exactly one teaspoon of icy electrolyte fluid every 10 minutes, using a timer. Do not exceed this volume. When you successfully keep this tiny amount down for an hour, consciously acknowledge that you are actively nourishing the pregnancy and reversing the dehydration, rewarding your strict adherence to this slow process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Requires urgent presentation to the Emergency Department or Obstetric Assessment Unit first thing in the morning for intravenous rehydration (Normal Saline with Potassium) and formal blood chemistry review. If oral intake remains impossible tonight, immediate ED transfer is required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you cannot keep a single teaspoon of fluid down for the next 4 hours, if you develop severe abdominal pain, if you experience any vaginal bleeding, or if you feel entirely unable to stand up without fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This severe form of pregnancy sickness requires close obstetric monitoring and intravenous fluid support to protect both maternal and fetal health.
Clinical Course: The extreme hormone levels of early pregnancy are causing a highly sensitive gag reflex and severe stomach slowing. This cycle of vomiting and starvation creates toxic ketones, which paradoxically worsen the nausea, making intravenous fluids often mandatory to break the cycle.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat any solid foods or drink plain water. Plain water will immediately trigger further vomiting. Stick strictly to the teaspoon-dosing of electrolyte solutions.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You are profoundly dehydrated and your body is burning its own fat, creating acids that make you feel even sicker. The sublingual wafer bypasses your stomach to turn off the nausea center in your brain. You must attend the hospital tomorrow for IV fluids to safely flush out these acids.
Actionable Steps: Take one teaspoon of electrolyte fluid every 10 minutes. Rest strictly in bed. Attend the hospital in the morning.

